00:00:09.966 --> 00:00:11.493
This is Priya Natarajan.

00:00:11.493 --> 00:00:14.480
I am the current director
of the Franke Program

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:17.620
and I'm delighted to invite you all

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:22.620
to the discussion session that
follows our talk yesterday.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:27.190
So yesterday we had as part of
our monthly colloquium series

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:30.370
in the understanding the
nature of inference project,

00:00:30.370 --> 00:00:34.910
we had a talk titled
experiments for causal inference

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:39.390
and system identification
by professor Nihat Ay.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:44.150
And as per custom, the day after the talk,

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:47.610
we have arranged to have
sort of a conversation

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:51.170
and a discussion between our speaker

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:53.610
and an invited interlocutor.

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:58.290
And I'm really thrilled that
the interlocutor we have today

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:01.790
to speak to professor Ay
is professor Kyle Cranmer

00:01:01.790 --> 00:01:03.820
who is a physicist

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:07.690
as well as someone who
is at the cutting edge

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:11.490
of applying data science,
and machine learning

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:15.420
to understanding sort of the
complex physics data landscape.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:20.420
His background is in particle
physics but I found...

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:22.520
For those of you who are interested,

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:24.260
we'll add a link to this.

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:26.550
In fact, Kyle is going
to be speaking later on

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:28.040
in our series as well

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:32.430
but he has a fabulous
paper that came out in PNAS

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:35.370
that really looks at the
question of inference

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:37.190
from simulations.

00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:40.150
And I learned a lot from that work

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:43.840
and I'm really thrilled to
have the two of you converse.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:48.840
So welcome Nihat, welcome
Kyle and take it away please.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:52.550
- Great, thank you very much.

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:55.540
So, yeah, so I put together a few slides

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:58.480
just to kind of kickstart the discussion

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:02.780
and also in case anyone wasn't able to see

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:03.910
all of the talk yesterday.

00:02:03.910 --> 00:02:06.720
So let me share those slides.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:08.070
I won't drone on for too long.

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:12.240
And then we can get to
the more interesting part

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:13.090
of the discussion

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:15.990
- What a gorgeous graphic Kyle.

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:17.953
- Thank you.

00:02:17.953 --> 00:02:21.080
Yeah, this is a collision event
at the Large Hadron Collider

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:23.180
where these very high
energy particles collide

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:24.480
in the center here

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:27.460
and all of these new
particles come flying out

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:28.320
into the detector.

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:30.090
And while it's a computer graphic,

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:32.910
it's a visualization
of an actual collision.

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:35.240
Ironically, it's with my
competitor experiment,

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:38.380
the CMS experiment while I
work on the ATLAS experiment

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:40.410
but they do have a good
graphics department

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:44.017
so (laughs) I'll take advantage of it.

00:02:44.017 --> 00:02:47.370
So this is a conversation
with professor Nihat Ay.

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:50.930
And so first I'll just start by, you know,

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:55.930
showing his outline and the
kind of flyer that we had.

00:02:56.570 --> 00:03:00.900
So again, this is part of this
multidisciplinary exploration

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:04.850
of the nature of inference,
correlation and causation.

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:07.500
I think it's a really
interesting a series of talks

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:10.118
that I'm very happy to be a part of it.

00:03:10.118 --> 00:03:13.120
And so professor Ay really
talked about, you know,

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:17.173
really the core canon
around causal inference.

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:19.810
And so the outline was here.

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:21.020
We didn't get to everything

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:22.850
so some of these really
interesting things at the end

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:24.190
we didn't get to

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:28.560
but we did get to go through
kind of like a lot of the core

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:32.340
about to this idea of
identifiability of causal effects.

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:36.430
And I'm not going to repeat
everything thing that he said

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:40.580
but I will just kind
of rewind a little bit

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:43.550
and say that when we're talking
about inference, you know,

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:46.130
there's a lot of times the
way that I'm thinking of it

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:48.960
as a physicist, we have
very predictive models

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:51.190
and those predictive models
are tied to this idea

00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:52.250
of mechanism.

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:56.060
And when we talk about causal
diagrams and things like this,

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:58.970
we're often talking
about mechanistic models.

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:02.120
And so it's really kind of
science in an old school way

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:03.730
where you really think you understand

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:05.420
what's going on underneath.

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:06.325
And so I think

00:04:06.325 --> 00:04:08.430
that the theory is basically
having some parameters.

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:09.820
Here I called them theta.

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:11.850
And for me, they might
be things like the mass

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:12.920
of the Higgs particle

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:15.550
or the cosmological constant or something.

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:17.860
And I'm trying to use
that theory to predict

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:19.940
what I would expect the data to look like.

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:22.540
And oftentimes that's
done probabilistically.

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:24.160
I'm not saying the data
is gonna be exactly this,

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:26.110
it's a distribution for the data.

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:27.560
And then given some observed data,

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:29.140
I wanna turn it around and do inference

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:30.380
on the parameters data.

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:31.670
And there we go.

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:34.340
You see how inference
is kind of this inverse

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:35.660
of the predictive direction.

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:38.910
And oftentimes people refer
to these as inverse problems.

00:04:38.910 --> 00:04:42.460
Now this is just kind of
Wikipedia level picture

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:44.020
of the scientific method.

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:46.380
Of course, you know,
inference in some sense

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:48.700
is just one piece of the scientific method

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:50.550
where we have some data in hand

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:53.650
and we're trying to infer
those parameters of the theory

00:04:53.650 --> 00:04:55.240
for whatever experiment

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:58.010
or set of observations
we happen to have done.

00:04:58.010 --> 00:05:00.390
Of course, the scientific
method is much more dynamic

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:03.210
where we're constantly
developing new hypotheses,

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:05.310
trying to figure out how to test them,

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:06.450
building new experiments.

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:07.990
So there's a lot more going on.

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:12.300
And so inference is really
kind of one particular aspect

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:13.933
of this process.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:19.880
But the role of theory
also kind of contrast

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:21.950
against a lot of the
buzz that was happening,

00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:24.180
I guess now it's been a few years.

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:28.820
This is a paper and
wired, I guess, from 2008

00:05:28.820 --> 00:05:31.420
but when big data was like all the rage,

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:32.260
there was this idea

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:36.502
that big data was going to
make theory irrelevant, right?

00:05:36.502 --> 00:05:38.860
And that the scientific
method would be obsolete.

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:41.297
And some of these ideas
were furthered by, you know,

00:05:41.297 --> 00:05:43.540
and these ideas were
like the fourth paradigm.

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:45.560
There's a famous quote from Peter Norvig

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:48.100
at Google talking about, you know,

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:50.190
to riff off of his famous quote

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:52.137
that all models are wrong and saying,

00:05:52.137 --> 00:05:53.360
"All models are wrong

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:56.200
and increasingly you can
get by without them."

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:57.910
And that's essentially saying

00:05:57.910 --> 00:05:59.060
that if you have a lot of data,

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:00.200
maybe you just don't need them.

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:03.280
Okay, and so this very
much comes from a mentality

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:05.510
that certainly you would
see in something like Google

00:06:05.510 --> 00:06:08.310
where your emphasis is largely
on prediction, you know,

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:10.360
given some data can I predict, you know,

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:13.360
someone's going to click on
an ad or something like that.

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:17.080
And now, so the kind
of counterpoint to that

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:21.780
has been, you know, led
largely by Judea Pearl

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:23.930
and, you know, many other people

00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:26.480
but he is written for instance
a very well-known book

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:27.960
called "The book of Y"

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:30.370
and he's, you know, done a very good job

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:33.803
of communicating the deficiencies

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:40.300
in this kind of purely data-driven way

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:42.530
of going about things.

00:06:42.530 --> 00:06:46.420
And he's described what he's
called the ladder of causation.

00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:48.980
And so there's sort of
three different rungs

00:06:48.980 --> 00:06:52.210
on this ladder, association, intervention

00:06:52.210 --> 00:06:53.170
and counterfactuals

00:06:53.170 --> 00:06:55.920
and professor Ay talked
a lot about intervention

00:06:57.060 --> 00:06:59.300
and somehow kind of a
little bit the relationship

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:00.670
between these two.

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:03.200
I'll just say a little bit
more about association.

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:08.200
So association to me is largely
how I thought of statistics

00:07:10.870 --> 00:07:13.780
prior to learning more
about causal inference

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:16.190
and the idea that you
have random variables

00:07:16.190 --> 00:07:21.190
and you observe them and
there's a distribution for them.

00:07:22.060 --> 00:07:23.880
And if you have many of them you want to,

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:26.610
for instance, understand
how they're correlated

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:28.430
and the best you could possibly do

00:07:28.430 --> 00:07:32.290
is know the exactly the joint
distribution over some data.

00:07:32.290 --> 00:07:33.530
And that would be maybe in the limit

00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:34.760
of an infinite amount of data.

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:36.240
So you're not talking about big data,

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:38.830
you're talking about like
the idealized infinite

00:07:38.830 --> 00:07:40.320
amount of data setting.

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:43.610
And what's become clear now
is that the types of questions

00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:47.260
that you can answer with
data alone are restricted

00:07:47.260 --> 00:07:49.350
and that there's a
broader class of questions

00:07:49.350 --> 00:07:50.680
that you can address

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:53.960
but to answer them, you
have to include data

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:55.110
and some assumptions

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:57.940
and those assumptions
can come in various forms

00:07:57.940 --> 00:07:59.490
but when you're talking
about causal inference,

00:07:59.490 --> 00:08:00.660
it's usually comes in the form

00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:03.133
of one of these causal graphs.

00:08:05.189 --> 00:08:09.960
And so I think some people
maybe wouldn't necessarily want

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:10.960
to accept this at first

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:13.070
but the the nice part of what's happening

00:08:13.070 --> 00:08:14.650
is you're getting just many examples

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:16.230
that make it kind of clear

00:08:16.230 --> 00:08:19.050
that there's no way you're
gonna be able to answer

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:20.190
these questions.

00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:21.023
So association--

00:08:21.023 --> 00:08:24.280
- So Kyle if I may ask for clarification.

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:29.280
So when mention theories previously

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:33.790
does theory include simulations as well

00:08:33.790 --> 00:08:36.000
like theoretical expectations

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:39.130
that are time evolved and so on?

00:08:39.130 --> 00:08:40.740
Are you really talking about,

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:42.140
so if you take particle physics,

00:08:42.140 --> 00:08:43.980
so if you're looking at
one of those collisions

00:08:43.980 --> 00:08:46.520
as our sort of test case,

00:08:46.520 --> 00:08:49.670
so would the theory be the standard model

00:08:49.670 --> 00:08:52.300
and the expectations of all the particles

00:08:52.300 --> 00:08:54.753
that you expect out of an interaction?

00:08:55.880 --> 00:08:59.727
I wanna understand, like what
is the starting point there?

00:08:59.727 --> 00:09:01.363
Can it be simulations?

00:09:02.620 --> 00:09:04.450
The other is the simulation,

00:09:04.450 --> 00:09:06.420
lattice gauge simulations,
for example, right?

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:10.850
So those are instantiations
of a theoretical prediction.

00:09:10.850 --> 00:09:13.170
So do you make a
distinction between the two

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:17.200
or are you gonna lump them
into one thing as theory?

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:18.860
- Yeah, I think you could.

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:22.660
Yeah, and here, I guess
maybe the label here

00:09:22.660 --> 00:09:23.520
is even kind of bad.

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:24.580
The theory in some sense,

00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:26.320
these are really just the
parameters of the theory

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:27.230
on the far left.

00:09:27.230 --> 00:09:32.230
To me I guess I would say
that simulation is oftentimes,

00:09:32.418 --> 00:09:33.980
like you have a theory,

00:09:33.980 --> 00:09:35.580
you may be able to write
it on a piece of paper

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:36.413
or something.

00:09:36.413 --> 00:09:40.100
It's usually some kind of
maybe some platonic ideal

00:09:41.087 --> 00:09:42.810
but the theory connected

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:47.750
with a concrete experimental scenario

00:09:47.750 --> 00:09:50.710
or observational scenario
should be the thing

00:09:50.710 --> 00:09:52.150
that allows you to make predictions

00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:54.100
for what you think the
data will look like.

00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:54.933
- Right.

00:09:54.933 --> 00:09:56.410
- And so then the way that you actually go

00:09:56.410 --> 00:09:57.610
about making the predictions,

00:09:57.610 --> 00:09:58.540
there's details there, you know,

00:09:58.540 --> 00:10:00.440
it might be a pencil
and paper calculation.

00:10:00.440 --> 00:10:01.750
it might be a simulation

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:07.490
and that's an important part

00:10:07.490 --> 00:10:10.210
and a lot of practical
issues arise in that.

00:10:10.210 --> 00:10:12.410
But to me I guess, I think of the theory

00:10:12.410 --> 00:10:14.093
as whatever it is that you need

00:10:14.093 --> 00:10:17.430
to be able to make
predictions about the data

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:21.950
when coupled with a concrete observational

00:10:21.950 --> 00:10:23.056
or experimental settings.

00:10:23.056 --> 00:10:25.093
- So I mean the reason I ask is right

00:10:25.093 --> 00:10:27.220
because if you look at
cosmology or astronomy

00:10:27.220 --> 00:10:28.890
versus particle physics

00:10:28.890 --> 00:10:32.880
one of the issues is the
particular epistemic role

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:34.550
of simulations themselves.

00:10:34.550 --> 00:10:38.600
We cannot do any controlled
experiments in cosmology, right?

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:39.660
And in particle physics,

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:41.700
you are able to do these
controlled experiments.

00:10:41.700 --> 00:10:45.160
So I was just very curious
whether these arrows

00:10:45.160 --> 00:10:48.590
that you sort of nicely
sketched out for us,

00:10:48.590 --> 00:10:50.610
they permit that flexibility

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:53.200
or is there something
different that happens

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:57.453
in a field when you can't replicate?

00:10:58.610 --> 00:11:00.260
- Yeah, no, I think a lot happens there

00:11:00.260 --> 00:11:04.080
and I didn't prepare
anything for that particular,

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:09.080
I mean, aspect of it and
let's see what you say.

00:11:09.770 --> 00:11:13.150
I mean, in a more refined
version of this picture,

00:11:13.150 --> 00:11:15.020
I would start to tease apart the theory

00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:17.840
into pieces that are like the
parts you really care about

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:21.620
and then parts that describe
whatever else you need

00:11:21.620 --> 00:11:23.160
to be able to make
predictions about the data.

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:24.830
So they may have to do
with your instrument.

00:11:24.830 --> 00:11:26.930
They may have to do with
a lot of, for instance,

00:11:26.930 --> 00:11:29.650
uncertain astrophysics that
you have no idea about.

00:11:29.650 --> 00:11:31.360
And so that starts to
introduce these ideas

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:34.300
of like nuisance parameters
and things like this.

00:11:34.300 --> 00:11:35.740
So to me in some sense

00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:37.940
the way I'm using the word theory loosely

00:11:37.940 --> 00:11:40.530
includes both the part
that you really care about

00:11:40.530 --> 00:11:42.990
as a scientist and all
the other disinter oughta

00:11:42.990 --> 00:11:47.990
that you need to include the,
make a prediction of the data.

00:11:48.370 --> 00:11:50.733
And that part might be
highly uncertain, right?

00:11:52.690 --> 00:11:54.460
And then difference about being able to do

00:11:54.460 --> 00:11:55.760
repeated experiments

00:11:55.760 --> 00:11:58.660
where you can actually kind
of control the circumstances

00:11:58.660 --> 00:12:02.010
versus kind of a purely
observational setting

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:08.610
that's also going to have a huge influence

00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:10.910
on what types of inference
you're going to be

00:12:10.910 --> 00:12:13.120
and it's going to limit you.

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:16.080
I don't know that I would
separate that really

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:18.120
from how I think about
the theory per say--

00:12:18.120 --> 00:12:18.953
- Okay.

00:12:20.090 --> 00:12:22.380
- But there's a lot less
you can do (laughs).

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:25.310
And that touches a little bit
on the ideas of interventions

00:12:25.310 --> 00:12:26.150
that we're getting to but--

00:12:26.150 --> 00:12:26.983
- Okay, great.

00:12:26.983 --> 00:12:29.900
No, I just wondered a
little bit of clarification

00:12:31.430 --> 00:12:36.430
to understand if this
looping and the connection,

00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:38.620
the way in which prediction

00:12:38.620 --> 00:12:43.250
and inference themselves
are related, alters,

00:12:43.250 --> 00:12:46.220
depending on if you have theories

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:52.560
that deal with data that
is not replicable, right?

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:54.900
So I was curious whether,

00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:58.180
but it sounds like these arrows

00:12:58.180 --> 00:13:01.380
and their directionality
remain as they are

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:04.710
but what you can infer
gets affected deeply

00:13:04.710 --> 00:13:05.957
depending on, okay--

00:13:05.957 --> 00:13:09.307
- Yeah, I think that's how
I would phrase it, you know,

00:13:09.307 --> 00:13:11.960
and in terms of like at the higher level

00:13:11.960 --> 00:13:14.390
not this inference task
but at the higher level

00:13:14.390 --> 00:13:16.970
like this kind of
scientific method approach.

00:13:16.970 --> 00:13:18.790
If you're able to repeat experiments

00:13:18.790 --> 00:13:19.870
or change circumstances

00:13:19.870 --> 00:13:22.890
obviously you're gonna
have a much more powerful

00:13:22.890 --> 00:13:24.600
set of tools available to you

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:26.920
but that already kind of
points at what's going on

00:13:26.920 --> 00:13:30.870
a little bit as if you think
of it at the association level

00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:32.360
is largely kind of this picture.

00:13:32.360 --> 00:13:34.630
You have data and you're kind of like

00:13:34.630 --> 00:13:37.870
if a theory gives you a joint
distribution on the data

00:13:37.870 --> 00:13:40.170
then you know everything
that there is to know

00:13:42.340 --> 00:13:45.200
and some sense there's
nothing more to the story.

00:13:45.200 --> 00:13:46.960
And if you had like an
infinite amount of data

00:13:46.960 --> 00:13:51.040
then like it should just reduce
to mathematics and calculus.

00:13:51.040 --> 00:13:53.410
It's not much more to the story

00:13:53.410 --> 00:13:54.590
but we somehow as scientists,

00:13:54.590 --> 00:13:56.040
we know there's a lot more to the story

00:13:56.040 --> 00:13:58.700
and your example about observational

00:13:58.700 --> 00:14:01.470
versus controlled experiments
points to the fact

00:14:01.470 --> 00:14:02.390
that they're different.

00:14:02.390 --> 00:14:04.220
But somehow that difference is not lying

00:14:04.220 --> 00:14:05.590
just in this picture.

00:14:05.590 --> 00:14:08.810
It's lying in the broader
setting of what can we do

00:14:08.810 --> 00:14:12.010
as scientists and yeah.

00:14:12.010 --> 00:14:14.170
- Great thanks for the clarification.

00:14:14.170 --> 00:14:17.590
- No, it's a great question

00:14:17.590 --> 00:14:20.633
and something that we could
talk about for hours and hours.

00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:22.790
So I don't wanna go on too long

00:14:22.790 --> 00:14:25.650
but I do wanna motivate a little bit more

00:14:25.650 --> 00:14:30.230
so this was kind of the big
data, early machine learning

00:14:30.230 --> 00:14:32.460
and also a lot of the data science

00:14:32.460 --> 00:14:34.660
and machine learning narratives
have been about, you know,

00:14:34.660 --> 00:14:35.870
around this kind of idea

00:14:35.870 --> 00:14:38.403
that maybe data can solve
all of our problems.

00:14:40.585 --> 00:14:42.185
And so, yeah, and as I mentioned

00:14:43.320 --> 00:14:45.460
in this ladder of causality

00:14:45.460 --> 00:14:47.710
that a lot of that
thinking is really stuck

00:14:47.710 --> 00:14:49.950
at the lowest rung here of association

00:14:49.950 --> 00:14:51.700
and that there kinds of questions

00:14:51.700 --> 00:14:53.707
you're basically just not
going to be able to answer

00:14:53.707 --> 00:14:55.330
and there's a broader set of questions

00:14:55.330 --> 00:14:57.070
that you're going to be able to address

00:14:57.070 --> 00:14:59.480
but they are going to kind
of require assumptions

00:14:59.480 --> 00:15:02.880
and those assumptions are usually encoded

00:15:02.880 --> 00:15:07.050
in the like a graphical
or a causal model, right?

00:15:07.050 --> 00:15:09.550
And so, okay, so just zooming in

00:15:09.550 --> 00:15:12.000
on kind of what kinds of
questions you're asking.

00:15:12.920 --> 00:15:16.580
And so associations are largely like

00:15:16.580 --> 00:15:19.710
if I knew X how does it
change my belief in Y?

00:15:19.710 --> 00:15:22.290
And those are things kind of
like conditional distributions

00:15:22.290 --> 00:15:26.010
and a lot of prediction
tasks can be cast this way

00:15:26.010 --> 00:15:28.600
but intervention is
asking a kind of question

00:15:28.600 --> 00:15:33.600
like what if I do something,
what's going to happen?

00:15:34.120 --> 00:15:36.810
So the example here is
if I take an aspirin

00:15:36.810 --> 00:15:38.950
will my headache be cured?

00:15:38.950 --> 00:15:40.370
You know, and like what will happen

00:15:40.370 --> 00:15:42.617
if you make a policy change
or something like that?

00:15:42.617 --> 00:15:44.860
And it could be something like, you know,

00:15:44.860 --> 00:15:46.740
I don't know what will happen
if I point my telescope

00:15:46.740 --> 00:15:49.700
in the side of the sky

00:15:49.700 --> 00:15:52.160
or what will happen if I change the energy

00:15:52.160 --> 00:15:54.840
of the beams colliding at
the Large Hadron Collider,

00:15:54.840 --> 00:15:55.673
things like this.

00:15:55.673 --> 00:15:57.720
And so you're going in
and you're intervening

00:15:57.720 --> 00:15:59.270
and you're doing something.

00:15:59.270 --> 00:16:01.300
And counterfactuals are
kind of another level

00:16:01.300 --> 00:16:02.650
that are very similar

00:16:02.650 --> 00:16:04.560
but the main difference here

00:16:04.560 --> 00:16:07.830
is that you're not necessarily able to go

00:16:07.830 --> 00:16:09.230
make that intervention

00:16:09.230 --> 00:16:10.830
like an examples that gave before,

00:16:10.830 --> 00:16:13.470
I can decide to take the aspirin or not.

00:16:13.470 --> 00:16:18.010
Here's an example that's like
if I had taken an aspirin,

00:16:18.010 --> 00:16:20.000
would it have stopped my headache?

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:21.610
And it sounds like it's just a grammar

00:16:21.610 --> 00:16:23.900
but the fact that it's
represented in our grammars

00:16:23.900 --> 00:16:26.210
shows how important it is, right?

00:16:26.210 --> 00:16:28.690
These are things that I can't do.

00:16:28.690 --> 00:16:30.773
I can't actually make this intervention.

00:16:32.360 --> 00:16:34.330
So how will you answer
a question like this

00:16:34.330 --> 00:16:35.500
is more difficult

00:16:35.500 --> 00:16:37.850
and it's a little bit
tied to like imagination.

00:16:38.900 --> 00:16:41.030
You're not going to actually collect data

00:16:41.030 --> 00:16:42.460
under these circumstances.

00:16:42.460 --> 00:16:44.037
You're going to have to
kind of imagine them.

00:16:44.037 --> 00:16:47.050
And that imagination does
kind of make you think

00:16:47.050 --> 00:16:49.000
about simulations a bit as well, right?

00:16:50.862 --> 00:16:51.840
So if you have a causal model,

00:16:51.840 --> 00:16:54.420
you can kind of start to
probe these sorts of questions

00:16:54.420 --> 00:16:55.610
but what you're gonna be able to do

00:16:55.610 --> 00:16:57.853
with actual data is gonna be restricted.

00:16:59.220 --> 00:17:01.210
And so then just to kind of
close the loop a little bit

00:17:01.210 --> 00:17:03.160
is if you look at thought leaders

00:17:04.048 --> 00:17:05.970
and for instance, deep learning,
I mean, machine learning

00:17:05.970 --> 00:17:08.100
like Yoshua Bengio, you know,

00:17:08.100 --> 00:17:10.280
they're looking at like
what are the bottlenecks

00:17:10.280 --> 00:17:11.323
for things like deep learning.

00:17:11.323 --> 00:17:14.120
It's not that they don't have enough data

00:17:14.120 --> 00:17:16.590
but you have things
like you're a classifier

00:17:16.590 --> 00:17:17.970
of if it's a cat or a dog

00:17:17.970 --> 00:17:22.510
or you're a automatic
driving car system might,

00:17:22.510 --> 00:17:24.410
you train it and it might work pretty well

00:17:24.410 --> 00:17:26.670
but then when you apply
it to real world images

00:17:26.670 --> 00:17:29.740
or real world data, it
might not perform well.

00:17:29.740 --> 00:17:32.680
And that might be because
that's what's sometimes referred

00:17:32.680 --> 00:17:35.270
to as a domain shift, okay?

00:17:35.270 --> 00:17:37.040
And so the question

00:17:37.040 --> 00:17:40.940
is if a lot of the machine learning things

00:17:40.940 --> 00:17:44.620
that just take advantage of correlations

00:17:44.620 --> 00:17:46.450
if you change the
distribution a little bit,

00:17:46.450 --> 00:17:48.940
they may behave in a way
that you don't want them to.

00:17:48.940 --> 00:17:50.750
And so the claim here is like, okay,

00:17:50.750 --> 00:17:53.500
well, what if we like structure
our machine learning things

00:17:53.500 --> 00:17:56.620
so that they have some causal mechanism

00:17:56.620 --> 00:17:58.940
and if we've got it
right then the hypothesis

00:17:58.940 --> 00:18:01.190
is that basically it's
gonna take less data

00:18:01.190 --> 00:18:02.850
and you're gonna be more robust

00:18:02.850 --> 00:18:06.510
and have all these great
things that people care about.

00:18:06.510 --> 00:18:07.860
And these are the things that are kind of

00:18:07.860 --> 00:18:11.100
on the way to something more
like artificial intelligence

00:18:11.100 --> 00:18:14.950
than just kind of a curve fitting picture

00:18:14.950 --> 00:18:16.513
of machine learning.

00:18:18.170 --> 00:18:21.500
So with that like a
little intro professor,

00:18:21.500 --> 00:18:26.500
I basically walked us through
a lot of the technology

00:18:28.500 --> 00:18:30.450
about how this works.

00:18:30.450 --> 00:18:33.090
Showed us graphical models.

00:18:33.090 --> 00:18:37.410
These Bayesian networks gave
us a mathematical formalism

00:18:37.410 --> 00:18:40.420
and introduced a lot of
the really core concepts.

00:18:40.420 --> 00:18:42.500
One of the points that he raised

00:18:42.500 --> 00:18:43.890
and I think this is one of the things

00:18:43.890 --> 00:18:44.723
that's really important

00:18:44.723 --> 00:18:47.500
is that when you think
about a data distribution,

00:18:47.500 --> 00:18:49.000
there's sort of two ways to think of it.

00:18:49.000 --> 00:18:52.730
One is to think of it as just
a big joint distribution,

00:18:52.730 --> 00:18:55.740
this kind of agnostic about
how the data got there.

00:18:55.740 --> 00:18:56.730
And the other way is to think

00:18:56.730 --> 00:18:58.610
of a data generating process right?

00:18:58.610 --> 00:19:01.360
So it can literally be time

00:19:01.360 --> 00:19:03.850
or it can be some abstraction of that

00:19:03.850 --> 00:19:07.120
but you have something
that's producing the data.

00:19:07.120 --> 00:19:08.380
And what's interesting

00:19:08.380 --> 00:19:13.380
is that here are three
different causal diagrams

00:19:14.160 --> 00:19:17.320
that all tell a different story
about how the data got there

00:19:17.320 --> 00:19:20.770
and they may have the
same joint distribution

00:19:20.770 --> 00:19:24.500
but the story, the narrative,
the mechanism is different

00:19:24.500 --> 00:19:25.610
in the different cases.

00:19:25.610 --> 00:19:28.920
And so the question is how
can I sort of tell them apart

00:19:28.920 --> 00:19:31.740
if ultimately I care about this mechanism?

00:19:31.740 --> 00:19:35.370
And so here I think a toy
example, it can be super useful.

00:19:35.370 --> 00:19:38.580
So took it from this blog
post which is really nice.

00:19:38.580 --> 00:19:40.930
And it's just making two-dimensional data.

00:19:40.930 --> 00:19:43.160
And I don't wanna scare
you with the programming

00:19:43.160 --> 00:19:45.460
but hopefully this is like simple enough

00:19:45.460 --> 00:19:48.030
that you have a random variable X

00:19:48.030 --> 00:19:51.000
and then you calculate a new variable Y

00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:53.330
and there's three different
various simple programs

00:19:53.330 --> 00:19:55.533
that make variables X and Y.

00:19:56.531 --> 00:19:59.100
And what's interesting is
they all give you exactly

00:19:59.100 --> 00:20:00.490
the same joint distribution.

00:20:00.490 --> 00:20:04.170
So from the point of view of
association, they're identical

00:20:04.170 --> 00:20:05.650
but they have three different stories

00:20:05.650 --> 00:20:07.080
about how you get there.

00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:08.700
And you might think, well, who cares,

00:20:08.700 --> 00:20:13.285
these are just like different
ways to make the data

00:20:13.285 --> 00:20:16.500
but they correspond to
different graphical models

00:20:16.500 --> 00:20:20.230
and these directed ones so
they have a causal story.

00:20:20.230 --> 00:20:24.060
And then professor, I
went through these ideas

00:20:24.060 --> 00:20:27.800
of the do-calculus and
interventions and the do-operation

00:20:27.800 --> 00:20:30.330
and tried to explain to us
like why that's different

00:20:30.330 --> 00:20:34.960
than just simply conditioning
and then also show

00:20:35.980 --> 00:20:39.170
that while you might be very
difficult to actually do

00:20:39.170 --> 00:20:40.060
those interventions,

00:20:40.060 --> 00:20:42.050
if you know something about this graph,

00:20:42.050 --> 00:20:44.010
you can kind of predict what would happen

00:20:44.010 --> 00:20:45.550
if you do an intervention.

00:20:45.550 --> 00:20:46.890
And it's exactly the fact

00:20:46.890 --> 00:20:49.200
that there are two different strategies.

00:20:49.200 --> 00:20:51.250
One which is actually
doing the intervention

00:20:51.250 --> 00:20:52.720
and seeing what happens

00:20:52.720 --> 00:20:54.700
and the other is that
you think you can predict

00:20:54.700 --> 00:20:56.030
what would happen

00:20:56.030 --> 00:20:59.460
if you know the underlying causal graph.

00:20:59.460 --> 00:21:01.230
That's kind of the important part, right?

00:21:01.230 --> 00:21:03.100
And so that's what you see here

00:21:03.100 --> 00:21:04.770
is that in some of these
equations on the left,

00:21:04.770 --> 00:21:07.220
you see for instance, the do operation

00:21:07.220 --> 00:21:09.440
and on the right you see things

00:21:09.440 --> 00:21:13.900
that are look more like
conditional distributions

00:21:13.900 --> 00:21:16.270
and things like this put
together in various ways.

00:21:16.270 --> 00:21:19.450
And so he went through a few
different examples of this

00:21:19.450 --> 00:21:21.640
and I'm not gonna go
through through all of that,

00:21:21.640 --> 00:21:24.070
just kind of reminding some plots.

00:21:24.070 --> 00:21:26.090
But in this toy example,
what's interesting

00:21:26.090 --> 00:21:28.990
and this also ties into simulations

00:21:28.990 --> 00:21:31.440
is that the way that you
can think about intervention

00:21:31.440 --> 00:21:35.640
is that okay, I'm going to
generate this random variable X

00:21:35.640 --> 00:21:38.010
and what I want to do is
understand the intervention

00:21:38.010 --> 00:21:39.530
where I set X equal to three.

00:21:39.530 --> 00:21:41.320
So that's this do operation.

00:21:41.320 --> 00:21:43.880
I'm gonna set X equal
to three to intervene,

00:21:43.880 --> 00:21:46.510
sort of like I'm gonna force
the person to take the aspirin.

00:21:46.510 --> 00:21:48.220
And what you would think of is like, okay,

00:21:48.220 --> 00:21:49.670
you generate the random X.

00:21:49.670 --> 00:21:52.330
Like I decide that maybe I
don't wanna take the aspirin

00:21:52.330 --> 00:21:53.330
but I'm gonna intervene

00:21:53.330 --> 00:21:55.310
and say, no, you are
taking the aspirin, right?

00:21:55.310 --> 00:21:57.610
So you're setting X equal to three.

00:21:57.610 --> 00:21:59.560
And then basically in
every step of the program,

00:21:59.560 --> 00:22:02.120
you're just gonna make
sure X is equal to three.

00:22:02.120 --> 00:22:05.740
And if you do that then the
distribution that you get out,

00:22:05.740 --> 00:22:07.420
you know, X is gonna be equal to three,

00:22:07.420 --> 00:22:08.740
no surprise there

00:22:08.740 --> 00:22:11.300
but the interesting part
is the distribution for Y

00:22:11.300 --> 00:22:13.570
is not the same in these different cases.

00:22:13.570 --> 00:22:16.103
And that's the statement
that the intervention,

00:22:17.353 --> 00:22:19.890
the do operation is not
the same as conditioning.

00:22:19.890 --> 00:22:22.723
And here you see it just
in a very explicit example.

00:22:25.520 --> 00:22:26.807
But this is a very simple one.

00:22:26.807 --> 00:22:29.300
And the question is how
do you scale this up?

00:22:29.300 --> 00:22:32.620
And this is where professor
Ay's research is really focused

00:22:32.620 --> 00:22:34.450
is all of these wonderful theorems

00:22:34.450 --> 00:22:36.470
that kind of talk about how to extend this

00:22:36.470 --> 00:22:39.160
even to very complicated situations.

00:22:39.160 --> 00:22:41.040
And I won't say more,

00:22:41.040 --> 00:22:43.690
I won't say this about counterfactuals.

00:22:43.690 --> 00:22:46.380
He did give some nice examples
about these like front door

00:22:46.380 --> 00:22:48.740
and back door examples.

00:22:48.740 --> 00:22:51.420
Gave examples in the context of robotics

00:22:51.420 --> 00:22:53.620
and artificial life.

00:22:53.620 --> 00:22:57.460
This example made me think of some work

00:22:57.460 --> 00:23:00.160
that I'd seen from DeepMind
on reinforcement learning.

00:23:01.430 --> 00:23:03.960
In reinforcement learning
they have like an agent,

00:23:03.960 --> 00:23:06.840
for instance, trying to explore some world

00:23:06.840 --> 00:23:09.910
that looks like a an
Atari game or something.

00:23:09.910 --> 00:23:13.880
And they had an example
where the training data

00:23:13.880 --> 00:23:17.490
is the way that the
reinforcement learning algorithms

00:23:17.490 --> 00:23:19.510
are usually trained is
at the association level.

00:23:19.510 --> 00:23:20.950
And it makes a mistake.

00:23:20.950 --> 00:23:22.030
And they tried to come in

00:23:22.030 --> 00:23:25.270
and fix that mistake through
this backdoor adjustment

00:23:25.270 --> 00:23:28.905
which is very much like what we see here.

00:23:28.905 --> 00:23:30.390
So this is just to kind of reinforce

00:23:30.390 --> 00:23:32.310
that these ideas of causal inference

00:23:32.310 --> 00:23:33.400
are really at the cutting edge

00:23:33.400 --> 00:23:35.010
of like way that people are thinking

00:23:35.010 --> 00:23:38.540
about moving machine learning
kind of from where it was

00:23:38.540 --> 00:23:43.020
with its first era of big
data and supervise learning

00:23:43.020 --> 00:23:45.950
and deep learning to
something a little bit more

00:23:45.950 --> 00:23:48.580
towards artificial
intelligence which is going,

00:23:48.580 --> 00:23:50.120
I think increasingly recognize

00:23:50.120 --> 00:23:53.840
that causal inference is gonna
be a major part of the story.

00:23:53.840 --> 00:23:56.240
So that was my little intro.

00:23:56.240 --> 00:23:58.683
I apologize if that went on too long.

00:23:59.800 --> 00:24:01.980
And so I'll stop there.

