So when you do in the context
of a generative model,

00:25:31.890 --> 00:25:33.637
so you have a neural network

00:25:33.637 --> 00:25:37.250
and the intention often is to...

00:25:37.250 --> 00:25:40.660
So you have the data
coming from the world.

00:25:40.660 --> 00:25:44.170
Okay, and let's talk about the sensors.

00:25:44.170 --> 00:25:45.170
So you have the sensors

00:25:45.170 --> 00:25:48.890
and you have some joint
distribution generated by the world.

00:25:48.890 --> 00:25:53.670
Okay, and you try to intrinsically
provide some explanation

00:25:53.670 --> 00:25:54.503
for that.

00:25:55.630 --> 00:25:57.330
The generative neural network

00:25:57.330 --> 00:26:01.430
let's say it's layout deep neural network

00:26:01.430 --> 00:26:03.960
and it's trained in a way

00:26:06.240 --> 00:26:08.970
that it can actually explain the data.

00:26:08.970 --> 00:26:11.480
So it can generate the same distribution

00:26:11.480 --> 00:26:15.220
as the distribution
generated by the world.

00:26:15.220 --> 00:26:19.245
And then the question is
to what extent can we...

00:26:19.245 --> 00:26:21.170
So on both sides of the sensors,

00:26:21.170 --> 00:26:25.330
so the world and the the neural
network can be represented

00:26:25.330 --> 00:26:27.090
in terms of a causal network.

00:26:27.090 --> 00:26:30.250
So neural network is a special
kind of causal network.

00:26:30.250 --> 00:26:32.420
And then there is a causal
network of the world

00:26:32.420 --> 00:26:34.670
that is generating the data.

00:26:34.670 --> 00:26:36.670
And we should not confuse

00:26:36.670 --> 00:26:40.980
and I think you implicitly
made that statement.

00:26:40.980 --> 00:26:43.613
We should not confuse the causal network,

00:26:45.550 --> 00:26:49.610
our ability to generate
the data from inside

00:26:49.610 --> 00:26:52.240
in terms of the neural network

00:26:52.240 --> 00:26:56.240
with the understanding of the
underlying causal structure

00:26:56.240 --> 00:26:57.073
of the world.

00:26:57.073 --> 00:26:58.860
So knowing that your network,

00:26:58.860 --> 00:27:00.530
the causal structure of the neural network

00:27:00.530 --> 00:27:04.250
does not tell us anything
about the causal structure

00:27:04.250 --> 00:27:05.290
of the world.

00:27:05.290 --> 00:27:10.290
So both of them are able to
generate the same distribution

00:27:10.697 --> 00:27:12.293
on the periphery so to say.

00:27:13.130 --> 00:27:15.950
And that's an interesting point.

00:27:15.950 --> 00:27:17.650
So that shouldn't be confuse.

00:27:17.650 --> 00:27:20.540
So if I'm able to
actually predict the data

00:27:20.540 --> 00:27:23.953
there might be many, many
possible solutions to that.

00:27:24.944 --> 00:27:28.850
And I think the world is one solution

00:27:28.850 --> 00:27:32.040
but the network itself
is also one solution.

00:27:32.040 --> 00:27:37.040
The theorem that I presented
at the end of my talk yesterday

00:27:37.940 --> 00:27:42.940
basically tells us what kind
of structural requirements

00:27:45.410 --> 00:27:49.094
we need in order to be able
to generate that distribution.

00:27:49.094 --> 00:27:49.927
- Right?

00:27:49.927 --> 00:27:54.560
- So if the generated
distribution has high redundancy,

00:27:55.700 --> 00:27:58.240
let's say a particular kind of redundancy

00:27:58.240 --> 00:28:01.120
that cannot be generated by any network.

00:28:01.120 --> 00:28:05.700
So it does tell us put some
constraints on both sides

00:28:05.700 --> 00:28:07.640
so to say off the periphery

00:28:07.640 --> 00:28:08.473
- Right.

00:28:08.473 --> 00:28:09.850
So Nihat had a quick question here.

00:28:09.850 --> 00:28:12.470
So I mean, the question often

00:28:12.470 --> 00:28:15.000
when we're dealing with
the kinds of data sets

00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:18.650
that we are dealing with
say, while modeling, right?

00:28:18.650 --> 00:28:21.960
Is the discrimination
between the solutions.

00:28:21.960 --> 00:28:24.110
And so one advantage in physics.

00:28:24.110 --> 00:28:26.060
So as I was talking about yesterday

00:28:26.060 --> 00:28:28.690
working on the black hole
growth problem or whatever.

00:28:28.690 --> 00:28:31.010
So you actually have physical principles

00:28:31.010 --> 00:28:34.290
that are going to constrain the world,

00:28:34.290 --> 00:28:38.300
sort of the map of
solutions that are offered.

00:28:38.300 --> 00:28:41.210
So some of them are
going to be unphysical,

00:28:41.210 --> 00:28:45.420
some of them are not going
to tally with cemeteries

00:28:45.420 --> 00:28:48.750
that physics, the underlying
fundamental physics.

00:28:48.750 --> 00:28:52.287
So do you think that
this inference process

00:28:55.130 --> 00:28:59.140
deeply depends on the
kind of data that we have

00:28:59.140 --> 00:29:01.670
and the relationship between the world

00:29:01.670 --> 00:29:05.190
and the neural network that
is getting the information

00:29:05.190 --> 00:29:06.400
from the world?

00:29:06.400 --> 00:29:08.960
Because in fields like physics,

00:29:08.960 --> 00:29:11.080
we know there are these principles, right?

00:29:11.080 --> 00:29:12.750
That constraint and restraint.

00:29:12.750 --> 00:29:14.130
So what is the role?

00:29:14.130 --> 00:29:18.960
I mean, do you see physical
laws as do functions

00:29:18.960 --> 00:29:21.000
or are they...

00:29:21.000 --> 00:29:22.500
What are they?

00:29:22.500 --> 00:29:24.250
- So that's a very important point.

00:29:28.380 --> 00:29:31.030
So I introduce the notion of a mechanism

00:29:32.260 --> 00:29:33.790
which is basically mark of counter

00:29:33.790 --> 00:29:36.160
but that can be any mark of counter.

00:29:36.160 --> 00:29:41.160
So I did not provide any
justification of a part,

00:29:41.270 --> 00:29:42.870
any distinction between physical

00:29:42.870 --> 00:29:44.660
and nonphysical Mark of counter.

00:29:44.660 --> 00:29:47.810
So all of them can be
realized in terms of physics

00:29:47.810 --> 00:29:52.810
but so observationally, let's
take the most simple case.

00:29:53.960 --> 00:29:55.080
You have X and Y

00:29:55.080 --> 00:29:56.970
and you have an arrow from X to Y.

00:29:56.970 --> 00:30:01.380
Observation you cannot
distinguish this from an arrow

00:30:01.380 --> 00:30:02.430
from Y to X.

00:30:02.430 --> 00:30:06.600
Okay, but if you compute
the condition distribution

00:30:06.600 --> 00:30:11.303
of Y given X and the condition
distribution of X given Y,

00:30:13.210 --> 00:30:17.040
you might have additional
information coming from physics

00:30:18.480 --> 00:30:22.350
which tells you that
one of these directions

00:30:22.350 --> 00:30:27.350
cannot be actually considered
as being physically plausible.

00:30:27.460 --> 00:30:29.550
That is an additional information

00:30:29.550 --> 00:30:33.630
that I did not incorporate
into my analysis yesterday

00:30:33.630 --> 00:30:35.893
but it plays an important role.

00:30:37.160 --> 00:30:38.390
So it could be for example,

00:30:38.390 --> 00:30:42.490
that you have a whole set of
observationally equivalent,

00:30:42.490 --> 00:30:45.300
mark of equivalent graphs

00:30:45.300 --> 00:30:49.210
but only some of them are
physically justifiable.

00:30:49.210 --> 00:30:50.043
- Right.

00:30:50.043 --> 00:30:52.890
- And that is actually
the basis of the group

00:30:52.890 --> 00:30:56.610
in Tübingen around Baden-Württemberg

00:30:58.470 --> 00:31:02.840
who basically say we have
to understand the primitive

00:31:02.840 --> 00:31:06.080
so to say, this X, Y example.

00:31:06.080 --> 00:31:11.080
And they basically argue
that the right direction

00:31:14.200 --> 00:31:16.140
has to have low complexity.

00:31:16.140 --> 00:31:18.560
So physics prefers low complexity.

00:31:18.560 --> 00:31:22.930
So you compute the P of Y
given X and the P of X given Y

00:31:22.930 --> 00:31:26.370
and then you can consider,
take for example,

00:31:26.370 --> 00:31:28.680
the mark of counter and
the commingle of complexity

00:31:28.680 --> 00:31:31.410
of that mark of that
counter of both of them

00:31:31.410 --> 00:31:34.435
and then you prefer the
one that has the smaller

00:31:34.435 --> 00:31:35.966
commingle of complexity.

00:31:35.966 --> 00:31:36.799
- And the criterion

00:31:36.799 --> 00:31:40.170
is that the smaller
commingle of complexity--

00:31:40.170 --> 00:31:42.440
- Complexity that, that would reflect

00:31:42.440 --> 00:31:45.210
the more physical directions.

00:31:45.210 --> 00:31:50.210
And that, of course, as you
say it's a further argument

00:31:51.200 --> 00:31:53.670
in order to be able to distinguish

00:31:53.670 --> 00:31:57.373
between observationally equivalent graphs.

00:31:58.780 --> 00:32:02.230
So I don't think that they
provide a physical argument

00:32:04.150 --> 00:32:06.290
but I think it's an important direction.

00:32:06.290 --> 00:32:09.830
So commingle of complexity
might not be the right quantity

00:32:09.830 --> 00:32:14.830
to take but I think the argument
may be related to something

00:32:18.500 --> 00:32:20.490
like the second law of thermodynamics--

00:32:20.490 --> 00:32:22.890
- I was just gonna say,
it sounds like entropy--

00:32:23.790 --> 00:32:27.340
- Yeah, so that would
allow you to actually

00:32:27.340 --> 00:32:28.920
distinguish further

00:32:30.020 --> 00:32:33.150
but that's a whole new argument

00:32:33.150 --> 00:32:35.300
that I did not talk about yesterday.

00:32:35.300 --> 00:32:36.600
- Right.

00:32:36.600 --> 00:32:38.433
Kyle, did you want to weigh in on this.

00:32:40.423 --> 00:32:44.460
- Yeah, I mean, well, the notion
of it being low complexity

00:32:44.460 --> 00:32:45.870
in some notion of complexity,

00:32:45.870 --> 00:32:48.210
I think is just a very natural version

00:32:48.210 --> 00:32:50.210
that's something like Occam's razor

00:32:50.210 --> 00:32:51.440
or minimum description length.

00:32:51.440 --> 00:32:53.000
It's like it has many, many forms

00:32:53.000 --> 00:32:55.110
and it's somewhat natural that, you know,

00:32:55.110 --> 00:32:57.880
it's basically just saying
if the fundamental mechanisms

00:32:57.880 --> 00:32:59.190
are simple.

00:32:59.190 --> 00:33:00.370
It gets a little complicated

00:33:00.370 --> 00:33:02.930
and that you might have very
simple fundamental mechanism

00:33:02.930 --> 00:33:06.030
but if you're not observing
at that granularity

00:33:06.030 --> 00:33:08.070
then the aggregate effect of that

00:33:08.070 --> 00:33:10.340
is could be some super complicated thing.

00:33:10.340 --> 00:33:14.980
And so then it's not clear
that the right causal arrow

00:33:14.980 --> 00:33:17.720
and the wrong causal arrow
is gonna manifest itself

00:33:17.720 --> 00:33:19.120
in terms of some complexity measure

00:33:19.120 --> 00:33:22.060
if you're not seeing like
the actual mechanism.

00:33:22.060 --> 00:33:25.943
But that's its own, that's a
very interesting discussion.

00:33:26.870 --> 00:33:31.240
One thing that I was going
to say and in my own kind of

00:33:31.240 --> 00:33:34.450
is I started going down the
rabbit hole of causal inference

00:33:34.450 --> 00:33:38.857
is just trying to understand
at a very, you know,

00:33:40.550 --> 00:33:44.840
almost like sociological
level as me as a physicist,

00:33:44.840 --> 00:33:47.810
how is it that I don't know
about causal inference?

00:33:47.810 --> 00:33:51.530
You know, the term causality is baked

00:33:51.530 --> 00:33:52.983
into being a physicist.

00:33:54.280 --> 00:33:57.810
And also so many of the
examples that you see,

00:33:57.810 --> 00:34:00.270
people go back to thinking
about physical examples

00:34:00.270 --> 00:34:02.870
or, you know, and talk about scientists.

00:34:02.870 --> 00:34:05.540
And so I was trying to reconcile,

00:34:05.540 --> 00:34:08.130
how is it that this thing
that seems to be so important

00:34:08.130 --> 00:34:09.100
for the scientific method

00:34:09.100 --> 00:34:11.610
and so closely related
to being a physicist

00:34:12.520 --> 00:34:16.420
is something that from a
mathematical point of view,

00:34:16.420 --> 00:34:18.570
don't feel, you know, it's not familiar?

00:34:18.570 --> 00:34:20.420
I was never taught about causal inferences

00:34:20.420 --> 00:34:22.230
in my physics classes.

00:34:22.230 --> 00:34:25.410
And so I think part of
that is that, you know,

00:34:25.410 --> 00:34:28.270
well, where I landed
on that is two things.

00:34:28.270 --> 00:34:31.610
One is that we take it
for almost for granted

00:34:31.610 --> 00:34:34.800
and it is so much a part of our thinking

00:34:34.800 --> 00:34:37.620
and it is very much a
part of why physicists

00:34:37.620 --> 00:34:38.580
have been successful.

00:34:38.580 --> 00:34:43.580
You know, if you think about
the scientific processes

00:34:43.640 --> 00:34:45.740
like the language of AI,

00:34:45.740 --> 00:34:47.450
we've had almost no data

00:34:47.450 --> 00:34:50.670
and almost no compute for the
history of human civilization

00:34:50.670 --> 00:34:53.350
to have learned so much about
how the universe works, right?

00:34:53.350 --> 00:34:55.150
And the only way that we got there

00:34:55.150 --> 00:34:57.913
was not through deep
learning or something, right?

00:34:58.824 --> 00:35:01.640
It was through exploiting causal structure

00:35:01.640 --> 00:35:03.100
and assumptions about causal structures.

00:35:03.100 --> 00:35:06.620
I might totally, you know, convinced

00:35:06.620 --> 00:35:08.390
that the only way that
we got to where we are

00:35:08.390 --> 00:35:10.930
is by exploiting that over
and over and over again.

00:35:10.930 --> 00:35:12.520
So it seems to me very important

00:35:12.520 --> 00:35:16.810
at the same time I don't
know about this language.

00:35:16.810 --> 00:35:19.040
I'm learning but like I
