00:36:03.590 --> 00:36:05.610
to be able to manipulate these things

00:36:05.610 --> 00:36:07.699
which like they're very counterintuitive

00:36:07.699 --> 00:36:08.940
a lot of times right?

00:36:08.940 --> 00:36:09.773
Even though--

00:36:09.773 --> 00:36:12.130
- You needed a very formal description.

00:36:12.130 --> 00:36:14.080
Yeah, yeah, you've sort
of really nailed it.

00:36:14.080 --> 00:36:19.080
I think in physics it's a
lot more intuitive in a way

00:36:19.090 --> 00:36:21.210
but for these other disciplines,

00:36:21.210 --> 00:36:23.790
you need to sort of
formalize the structure

00:36:24.755 --> 00:36:25.790
and order it, right?

00:36:25.790 --> 00:36:26.623
And order it.

00:36:26.623 --> 00:36:29.010
As you said, one of the interesting things

00:36:29.010 --> 00:36:30.370
in the example that you gave,

00:36:30.370 --> 00:36:32.920
I mean, there is never ambiguity.

00:36:32.920 --> 00:36:36.010
It is never a question that
once the photons are released

00:36:36.010 --> 00:36:38.480
all the filters through
which they have to go through

00:36:38.480 --> 00:36:39.690
to reach us, right?

00:36:39.690 --> 00:36:43.070
And the fact what follows what

00:36:43.070 --> 00:36:46.133
is a pretty patternly clear, right?

00:36:46.133 --> 00:36:47.616
- Yeah, I think so.

00:36:47.616 --> 00:36:52.616
And then, yeah, so I think
that part's interesting.

00:36:53.380 --> 00:36:55.870
I can say there are a few other things

00:36:55.870 --> 00:36:56.740
from a physics point of view

00:36:56.740 --> 00:36:59.210
that they become interesting
for causal inference.

00:36:59.210 --> 00:37:01.280
So one of them I'll just mention

00:37:03.054 --> 00:37:06.610
is that if you think about
something like thermodynamics

00:37:06.610 --> 00:37:07.773
and equilibrium,

00:37:09.000 --> 00:37:10.320
that's a situation we're like

00:37:10.320 --> 00:37:14.640
you have like the ideal gas
law, like pressure and volume

00:37:14.640 --> 00:37:18.070
and the temperature,
you know, PV equals nRT.

00:37:18.070 --> 00:37:19.170
And it's just an equation.

00:37:19.170 --> 00:37:22.380
And so many things in physics
are written as equations,

00:37:22.380 --> 00:37:23.430
E equals MC squared.

00:37:23.430 --> 00:37:24.730
You know, they're all there

00:37:24.730 --> 00:37:27.350
and there's no causal direction there.

00:37:27.350 --> 00:37:29.110
And I don't just mean in the deeper sense

00:37:29.110 --> 00:37:31.840
that like our first talk about
kind of the arrow of time

00:37:31.840 --> 00:37:34.380
but I just mean it's
just an equality, right?

00:37:34.380 --> 00:37:36.100
And so I think it's interesting

00:37:36.100 --> 00:37:39.210
because A, I think a lot of
times when we write equations

00:37:39.210 --> 00:37:42.450
like that, we're basically
making a mistake.

00:37:42.450 --> 00:37:43.470
When we write those equations,

00:37:43.470 --> 00:37:46.010
we really mean something
like the inferred value

00:37:46.010 --> 00:37:48.900
from these other things are equal

00:37:48.900 --> 00:37:51.090
and like almost like a
maximum likelihood sense.

00:37:51.090 --> 00:37:52.990
But the other part is that the...

00:37:54.357 --> 00:37:56.370
And something like, you
know, if you push down

00:37:56.370 --> 00:37:59.350
on the piston and the volume goes down

00:37:59.350 --> 00:38:00.410
and the temperature goes up,

00:38:00.410 --> 00:38:02.640
like who's causing what.

00:38:02.640 --> 00:38:06.263
And it's pretty clear
you're pushing on it, right?

00:38:07.850 --> 00:38:09.560
But in other cases like I heat it up

00:38:09.560 --> 00:38:10.820
and then the piston goes up, right?

00:38:10.820 --> 00:38:11.920
It's the same relationship

00:38:11.920 --> 00:38:15.770
but the causal story goes kind
of in a different direction.

00:38:15.770 --> 00:38:18.080
And what's a little
interesting to me there

00:38:18.080 --> 00:38:20.230
is that it goes back to the first talk.

00:38:20.230 --> 00:38:22.880
If you think about core screenings
and macroscopic structure

00:38:22.880 --> 00:38:25.180
versus microscopic structure, you know,

00:38:25.180 --> 00:38:27.830
a lot of the way that
that first seminar was,

00:38:27.830 --> 00:38:31.150
was we see causality and the macroscopic

00:38:31.150 --> 00:38:34.000
but when you go to the micro, sorry,

00:38:34.000 --> 00:38:36.080
you see causality and
the macroscopic world

00:38:36.080 --> 00:38:38.090
but when you go to the microscopic world,

00:38:38.090 --> 00:38:41.660
it goes away cause you have
the time reversible dynamics

00:38:41.660 --> 00:38:45.350
but the equilibrium one is a
little bit the opposite story.

00:38:45.350 --> 00:38:47.940
The macroscopic story is
kind of not really clear

00:38:47.940 --> 00:38:50.730
what the causal thing is cause
you're always in equilibrium

00:38:50.730 --> 00:38:53.133
and there's no real causal
graph that's obvious.

00:38:54.100 --> 00:38:55.360
But if you could zoom in

00:38:55.360 --> 00:38:57.310
and see like the atoms bouncing around

00:38:57.310 --> 00:38:59.280
or something like that, you know,

00:38:59.280 --> 00:39:01.540
at some mesoscopic course grading

00:39:01.540 --> 00:39:03.730
you would see like which way, you know,

00:39:03.730 --> 00:39:04.607
who's causing what, right?

00:39:04.607 --> 00:39:07.000
You know, you're out of
equilibrium for a little bit

00:39:07.000 --> 00:39:08.500
and you know what's happening.

00:39:09.500 --> 00:39:13.330
So I think that part as a
physicist is kind of interesting.

00:39:13.330 --> 00:39:16.270
And then the other
question is like, you know,

00:39:16.270 --> 00:39:17.170
in the do-calculus,

00:39:17.170 --> 00:39:20.220
what if I wanted to like intervene

00:39:20.220 --> 00:39:22.750
and set like the energy
of a particle to something

00:39:22.750 --> 00:39:25.760
without modifying its momentum, right?

00:39:25.760 --> 00:39:29.660
If I did that, like I would break the laws

00:39:29.660 --> 00:39:31.910
that say how energy and
momentum are related,

00:39:31.910 --> 00:39:32.960
the on-shell conditions.

00:39:32.960 --> 00:39:35.760
So there are a lot of
interventions that don't, you know,

00:39:35.760 --> 00:39:37.460
if I had a simulator in principal,

00:39:37.460 --> 00:39:39.680
I could go in there and
change those numbers

00:39:39.680 --> 00:39:41.770
but they don't make any sense physically.

00:39:41.770 --> 00:39:45.360
So in addition to the causal
arrows maybe being physical

00:39:45.360 --> 00:39:50.360
or not, some interventions
don't seem realizable.

00:39:50.530 --> 00:39:53.240
And so I'm not part of the discussion.

00:39:53.240 --> 00:39:54.160
I don't know much about it.

00:39:54.160 --> 00:39:56.640
But I know there's a lot of
like philosophical debates

00:39:56.640 --> 00:39:59.740
about interventions in the do-calculus.

00:39:59.740 --> 00:40:00.650
And what does it mean

00:40:00.650 --> 00:40:02.890
if you can't actually
make that intervention?

00:40:02.890 --> 00:40:04.920
So I'd be curious to
hear a professor Ay talk

00:40:04.920 --> 00:40:05.753
about that a little bit

00:40:05.753 --> 00:40:07.340
cause I don't really know about it.

00:40:08.980 --> 00:40:13.430
- Yeah, so that's a
very interesting subject

00:40:13.430 --> 00:40:16.970
and I don't think it's fully resolved.

00:40:16.970 --> 00:40:19.570
So there is, of course, in physics,

00:40:19.570 --> 00:40:24.270
not only directed in interaction

00:40:24.270 --> 00:40:28.770
but also on undirected
like the Ising model,

00:40:28.770 --> 00:40:30.740
you have undirected interactions.

00:40:30.740 --> 00:40:35.610
And so that's one thing
I didn't talk about that.

00:40:35.610 --> 00:40:40.100
There is some work trying
to actually explain

00:40:40.100 --> 00:40:42.340
undirected interactions

00:40:42.340 --> 00:40:44.916
in terms of many, many, many direct

00:40:44.916 --> 00:40:46.336
and indirect interactions.

00:40:46.336 --> 00:40:48.433
So like integrated phenomenon so to say.

00:40:49.510 --> 00:40:53.367
But the theory is not--

00:41:01.688 --> 00:41:02.951
- It's not settled, right?

00:41:02.951 --> 00:41:05.020
The question is not settled.

00:41:05.020 --> 00:41:05.890
- It's not settled.

00:41:05.890 --> 00:41:08.070
So that's one thing, it's not settled.

00:41:08.070 --> 00:41:10.850
But then there's also physical constraints

00:41:11.770 --> 00:41:15.380
which is maybe special case
of functional constraints.

00:41:15.380 --> 00:41:16.870
So you cannot change one variable

00:41:16.870 --> 00:41:18.910
without changing the other one.

00:41:18.910 --> 00:41:21.290
And that's exactly the problem that we had

00:41:21.290 --> 00:41:22.790
in the context of robotics.

00:41:22.790 --> 00:41:25.340
So we have really constraints,
physical constraints

00:41:26.548 --> 00:41:31.548
and physical constraints
imply that many configurations

00:41:38.030 --> 00:41:40.310
have zero probability.

00:41:40.310 --> 00:41:45.310
Okay, and this is why you
cannot actually compute

00:41:46.610 --> 00:41:48.030
the conditional distribution.

00:41:48.030 --> 00:41:52.490
And as I said yesterday, so
this identifiability problem

00:41:54.210 --> 00:41:56.240
really requires strict positivity

00:41:57.158 --> 00:41:58.760
of the joint distribution.

00:41:58.760 --> 00:42:01.433
And when you have these
physical constraints,

00:42:03.100 --> 00:42:05.830
you don't have this strict positivity

00:42:05.830 --> 00:42:07.730
so they don't hold anymore.

00:42:07.730 --> 00:42:12.270
This identifiability equations
that I presented yesterday.

00:42:12.270 --> 00:42:15.763
So it's a limitation of
the theory, I would say.

00:42:17.440 --> 00:42:21.840
- Yeah, I mean, going back
to Colonel Bradley's talk

00:42:21.840 --> 00:42:23.279
in the first one.

00:42:23.279 --> 00:42:25.860
He also made this point about agency

00:42:25.860 --> 00:42:27.810
and I sort of like going
in and changing things.

00:42:27.810 --> 00:42:32.190
And I think, if you go all
the way down the rabbit hole

00:42:32.190 --> 00:42:33.820
of thinking about fundamental physics

00:42:33.820 --> 00:42:38.140
and things being time
reversible and et cetera,

00:42:38.140 --> 00:42:39.550
it almost turns into the point

00:42:39.550 --> 00:42:41.070
that basically none of the interventions

00:42:41.070 --> 00:42:41.950
are gonna be possible

00:42:41.950 --> 00:42:44.630
because everything is so tied together

00:42:44.630 --> 00:42:46.550
that you can't move one thing without...

00:42:46.550 --> 00:42:49.940
And so it seems like it points to,

00:42:49.940 --> 00:42:50.890
at the end of the road,

00:42:50.890 --> 00:42:52.350
that you're not going to really be able

00:42:52.350 --> 00:42:53.540
to do such an intervention

00:42:53.540 --> 00:42:55.920
unless you can only consider a subsystem.

00:42:55.920 --> 00:42:57.390
And that was exactly I think his point

00:42:57.390 --> 00:42:59.400
is that if you consider it a subsystem,

00:42:59.400 --> 00:43:00.990
so you can change something internally

00:43:00.990 --> 00:43:05.970
and something externally
has to react for consistency

00:43:07.460 --> 00:43:09.810
then you start seeing those
kinds of interventions

00:43:09.810 --> 00:43:12.810
and a lot of ideas about
causality start to emerge

00:43:12.810 --> 00:43:14.960
and that can be somewhat
of the resolution.

00:43:16.103 --> 00:43:18.940
- I think he also briefly
alluded to the fact

00:43:18.940 --> 00:43:22.190
that or if you could make a complete map

00:43:22.190 --> 00:43:24.620
of all interferences

00:43:24.620 --> 00:43:29.532
like all the existing correlates
between variables, right?

00:43:29.532 --> 00:43:33.100
So cause when he was trying
to contrast the quantum case

00:43:33.100 --> 00:43:34.580
from sort of classical cases,

00:43:34.580 --> 00:43:37.610
he was saying, well, you
know, the classical case,

00:43:37.610 --> 00:43:42.610
you can actually map out what
all changes deterministically

00:43:43.000 --> 00:43:44.730
when you change one thing

00:43:44.730 --> 00:43:46.230
but as in quantum mechanics

00:43:46.230 --> 00:43:48.963
that itself is not often well-defined.

00:43:52.210 --> 00:43:53.220
So yeah.

00:43:53.220 --> 00:43:54.550
So yeah

00:43:55.910 --> 00:43:58.100
- One point I also wanted to comment

00:43:58.100 --> 00:44:00.910
on this notion of agency.

00:44:00.910 --> 00:44:05.360
I think that was not highlighted
in the previous talk.

00:44:05.360 --> 00:44:08.030
So when you do intervention,

00:44:08.030 --> 00:44:11.400
it is really important that the
intervention is independent.

00:44:11.400 --> 00:44:13.720
So the agent who is
intervening is independent

00:44:13.720 --> 00:44:16.483
of the system, statistically independent,

00:44:18.639 --> 00:44:23.517
otherwise you don't get the
results that I have presented.

00:44:25.480 --> 00:44:28.700
So intervention really has to assume

00:44:28.700 --> 00:44:30.950
an agent sitting outside

00:44:30.950 --> 00:44:35.790
and really deciding on
the state to intervene.

00:44:35.790 --> 00:44:40.350
So that sets some constraints on agency.

00:44:40.350 --> 00:44:44.360
On the other hand, the
creatures that we studied

00:44:44.360 --> 00:44:46.080
in this robotic setting,

00:44:46.080 --> 00:44:50.060
these are agents, so there's agency

00:44:50.060 --> 00:44:51.920
but these agents really,

00:44:51.920 --> 00:44:54.750
before they act they
receive some information

00:44:54.750 --> 00:44:58.410
from the environment through
their sensors and then they act

00:44:58.410 --> 00:45:00.980
and that's a kind of a
disconnect with the theory.

00:45:00.980 --> 00:45:03.410
So our agents are--

00:45:04.360 --> 00:45:08.240
- But don't they have a
set of formal instructions

00:45:08.240 --> 00:45:11.700
already specified if they sense X,

00:45:11.700 --> 00:45:14.700
they can do Y1, Y2 or Y3.

00:45:14.700 --> 00:45:15.840
It's finite.

00:45:15.840 --> 00:45:20.670
If they sense Z, they can
do three possibilities.

00:45:20.670 --> 00:45:24.453
So I mean, how is that independent?

00:45:25.460 --> 00:45:27.310
Because they are already
instructed, right?

00:45:27.310 --> 00:45:28.397
I mean, there's a--

00:45:28.397 --> 00:45:29.230
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:45:29.230 --> 00:45:30.660
So that's, that's what I'm saying.

00:45:30.660 --> 00:45:32.588
They are not independent.

00:45:32.588 --> 00:45:33.421
- Okay.

00:45:33.421 --> 00:45:38.270
- And we call them agents and
they would not be qualified

00:45:38.270 --> 00:45:41.500
this way of lookup table
of which action to choose

00:45:41.500 --> 00:45:46.210
based on which sensory input
would not be consistent

00:45:47.921 --> 00:45:48.860
with the notion of intervention

00:45:48.860 --> 00:45:50.373
that I talked about yesterday.

00:45:51.680 --> 00:45:53.460
Yeah so...

00:45:56.047 --> 00:46:00.130
- Yeah, probably some fine
grained language there

00:46:01.030 --> 00:46:02.280
is it necessary (laughs)?

00:46:03.930 --> 00:46:04.763
- Yeah

00:46:04.763 --> 00:46:08.090
- Yeah, I think at this point
we can open up to questions.

00:46:08.090 --> 00:46:10.690
If there are any questions
from the audience

00:46:10.690 --> 00:46:13.060
please feel free to indicate in the chat

00:46:13.060 --> 00:46:15.900
that you want to ask the
question and we can unmute you

00:46:15.900 --> 00:46:18.750
- So may I also suggest.

00:46:18.750 --> 00:46:21.680
So one thing, I wanted to actually try

00:46:21.680 --> 00:46:25.350
to draw a little bit the kind of reasoning

00:46:25.350 --> 00:46:30.350
that I presented yesterday
maybe as a starting point

00:46:30.490 --> 00:46:32.950
for further questions or discussion.

00:46:32.950 --> 00:46:33.840
- That'd be great.

00:46:33.840 --> 00:46:38.770
- So let me try to connect my iPad.

00:46:38.770 --> 00:46:39.603
- Yeah.

00:46:42.179 --> 00:46:46.197
- It's a modification of the
diagram that we just saw.

00:46:49.310 --> 00:46:51.620
(all laugh)

00:46:51.620 --> 00:46:55.420
Okay, so basically what I wanted to show,

00:46:55.420 --> 00:46:57.610
so we have this diagram here.

00:46:57.610 --> 00:46:58.700
- Oh, this is wonderful.

00:46:58.700 --> 00:47:00.860
I was just saying, we miss a blackboard.

00:47:00.860 --> 00:47:01.880
So this is great.

00:47:01.880 --> 00:47:06.880
- Yeah, so we had this
prediction and this is the data.

00:47:13.840 --> 00:47:15.453
This is the theory.

00:47:18.180 --> 00:47:19.973
And then we had inference.

00:47:22.600 --> 00:47:24.330
So before I saw this diagram,

00:47:24.330 --> 00:47:26.140
I actually prepared another one

00:47:27.290 --> 00:47:31.010
in order to illustrate what
I have presented yesterday.

00:47:31.010 --> 00:47:34.523
So basically we have a system.

00:47:39.700 --> 00:47:41.500
And we want to understand that system,

00:47:41.500 --> 00:47:43.670
the inner working of that system.

00:47:43.670 --> 00:47:48.653
So we do experiments and these
experiments generate data.

00:47:55.710 --> 00:47:59.620
And with the data, we can
actually do the inference,

00:47:59.620 --> 00:48:01.640
like we denote this by question mark.

00:48:01.640 --> 00:48:03.770
So that's the general paradigm.

00:48:03.770 --> 00:48:07.890
So I think it was referred
to as the inverse problem,

00:48:07.890 --> 00:48:10.200
inference as the inverse
problem from the data.

00:48:10.200 --> 00:48:14.183
And now the question is
what kind of experiments?

00:48:15.883 --> 00:48:18.390
So now I'm going to
specialize this a little bit.

00:48:18.390 --> 00:48:23.060
So the system was bottled in
terms of a causal network.

00:48:27.143 --> 00:48:31.670
And then I presented yesterday
three kinds of experiments.

00:48:31.670 --> 00:48:36.670
One is simply observation and
this creates one type of data.

00:48:41.300 --> 00:48:43.570
Let's call it data one

00:48:43.570 --> 00:48:45.700
and you can ask the
question what can I get

00:48:45.700 --> 00:48:48.410
from just observing the system?

00:48:48.410 --> 00:48:51.640
So that's one way to do experiments.

00:48:51.640 --> 00:48:55.800
The second one is intervention

00:48:58.234 --> 00:49:01.050
and that generates a second kind of data.

00:49:03.200 --> 00:49:05.310
And you can ask the same question.

00:49:05.310 --> 00:49:09.200
And the thing that I had
no time to talk about it

00:49:09.200 --> 00:49:12.603
is sometimes you do
experiments, for example,

00:49:14.300 --> 00:49:17.000
in the context of genotype,
phenotype mapping,

00:49:17.000 --> 00:49:18.450
you do knockout experiments.

00:49:18.450 --> 00:49:19.470
You'll knock out genes

00:49:19.470 --> 00:49:22.610
in order to understand
the function of the gene.

00:49:22.610 --> 00:49:25.940
And that's some kind of intervention

00:49:25.940 --> 00:49:28.930
but basically it's a kind
of structural intervention.

00:49:28.930 --> 00:49:33.930
You remove parts of the system
to see the role of the gene,

00:49:34.290 --> 00:49:36.930
you remove the gene or block the gene.

00:49:36.930 --> 00:49:41.930
And then if you see an effect,
a change of the phenotype

00:49:42.410 --> 00:49:47.180
you say, ah, that gene was
responsible for this phenotype.

00:49:47.180 --> 00:49:50.500
And that was what I wanted
to talk about yesterday.

00:49:50.500 --> 00:49:52.533
So that's a knockout.

00:49:53.400 --> 00:49:56.460
- So Nihat here I want you to clarify,

00:49:56.460 --> 00:49:59.280
so these cosmological
simulations that we do.

00:49:59.280 --> 00:50:02.060
So I want both of you to clarify for me

00:50:02.060 --> 00:50:04.190
which kind of interventions are there?

00:50:04.190 --> 00:50:06.860
Because I can see them
being a normal intervention

00:50:06.860 --> 00:50:10.370
or a knockout because I
can choose parameters.

00:50:10.370 --> 00:50:13.810
There are a few numbers that
characterize a typical universe

00:50:13.810 --> 00:50:15.950
and then you can evolve them.

00:50:15.950 --> 00:50:19.000
And I can choose a
different set of parameters

00:50:19.000 --> 00:50:21.380
and evolve different universes.

00:50:21.380 --> 00:50:23.240
So in that sense, it's knockout

00:50:23.240 --> 00:50:28.120
because I can by hand fix values

00:50:28.120 --> 00:50:31.290
but then is it an intervention
or is it a knockout?

00:50:31.290 --> 00:50:33.470
- I think a knock out
would be maybe more like

00:50:33.470 --> 00:50:35.550
if you were trying to
understand, you know,

00:50:35.550 --> 00:50:39.210
say how black hole, I mean,
black dark matters, sorry,

00:50:39.210 --> 00:50:40.920
dark matter clumps in the universe

00:50:40.920 --> 00:50:44.160
and the role of like gas and
geons and stuff like that.

00:50:44.160 --> 00:50:48.330
If you just took out the gas
and re-ran the simulation

00:50:48.330 --> 00:50:50.653
that's sort of like
removing a whole mechanism

00:50:50.653 --> 00:50:53.200
that seems to closer to a knockout to me.

00:50:53.200 --> 00:50:54.870
The other one where you're
just running the simulation

00:50:54.870 --> 00:50:56.630
with different values of the parameters.

00:50:56.630 --> 00:50:59.660
To me that's a, well, I mean,

00:50:59.660 --> 00:51:02.010
it's sort of making a
prediction for different values

00:51:02.010 --> 00:51:03.890
of the parameters of the theory.

00:51:03.890 --> 00:51:06.585
If you want it, you can
think of that as a--

00:51:06.585 --> 00:51:07.760
- Intervention.

00:51:07.760 --> 00:51:08.593
- Well, yeah.

00:51:08.593 --> 00:51:09.760
And then there's a
question is in intervention

00:51:09.760 --> 00:51:12.784
or is it like just conditioning?

00:51:12.784 --> 00:51:15.480
But usually those
parameters are at the top

00:51:15.480 --> 00:51:16.537
of the causal model, right?

00:51:16.537 --> 00:51:19.040
And if I, professor, I can, correct me

00:51:19.040 --> 00:51:21.290
but one of the examples he gave

00:51:21.290 --> 00:51:22.450
if you intervene at the thing,

00:51:22.450 --> 00:51:25.667
that's the very apparent then conditioning

00:51:25.667 --> 00:51:27.360
and interventions are the same.

00:51:27.360 --> 00:51:29.093
So then there's no distinction.

00:51:30.280 --> 00:51:32.800
- Yeah, I think that's what I got too.

00:51:32.800 --> 00:51:33.963
Is that right Nihat?

00:51:34.990 --> 00:51:36.556
- Yes, I wouldn't say so, yeah.

00:51:36.556 --> 00:51:39.403
- Okay, but then if I change the nature

00:51:39.403 --> 00:51:42.570
of dark matter itself would that be...

00:51:44.340 --> 00:51:45.173
- Yeah, I think there you get

00:51:45.173 --> 00:51:48.360
into a question of when
you think about theories

00:51:48.360 --> 00:51:51.580
do you think of them as
like continuous parameters

00:51:51.580 --> 00:51:54.510
and if you have two different theories

00:51:54.510 --> 00:51:55.910
it's like a discrete choice,

00:51:57.601 --> 00:51:58.770
you can either think of them
as two different theories

00:51:58.770 --> 00:51:59.900
or you can think of it as one theory

00:51:59.900 --> 00:52:02.730
with like a discreet parameter.

00:52:02.730 --> 00:52:05.760
But like to me, the structure of inference

00:52:05.760 --> 00:52:08.969
and conditioning and
do-operations aren't changed.

00:52:08.969 --> 00:52:09.802
- Have not changed, okay?

00:52:09.802 --> 00:52:11.960
- It's more about discrete and continuous.

00:52:13.130 --> 00:52:13.963
- Okay.

00:52:16.040 --> 00:52:18.740
- So maybe we can move to
the questions (giggles).

00:52:19.952 --> 00:52:22.460
- Yeah, no, but this was really nice,

00:52:22.460 --> 00:52:27.460
the clarification of
the three kinds of ways

00:52:28.970 --> 00:52:32.573
of generating data and
therefore the inference loops.

00:52:33.520 --> 00:52:35.540
So to finish up, you were saying that...

00:52:35.540 --> 00:52:39.620
So your argument is that
the way inference works

00:52:39.620 --> 00:52:43.913
in these three kinds of
causal systems is different.

00:52:45.430 --> 00:52:49.490
- So my argument is that in order

00:52:49.490 --> 00:52:51.930
to reveal the inner working of the system,

00:52:51.930 --> 00:52:53.520
you can't just observe it,

00:52:53.520 --> 00:52:57.943
you need to apply different
kinds of experiments.

00:53:00.420 --> 00:53:03.850
And I got into this actually when I went

00:53:03.850 --> 00:53:06.400
to the Santa Fe Institute as a post-doc

00:53:06.400 --> 00:53:09.090
and there I met Jessica Flack

00:53:09.090 --> 00:53:13.730
who was studying primate societies

00:53:13.730 --> 00:53:17.160
and she was actually liking
the gene knockout experiment.

00:53:17.160 --> 00:53:20.990
She was actually removing
some of these individuals

00:53:20.990 --> 00:53:23.730
from the society and recording the changes

00:53:23.730 --> 00:53:25.770
of the behavior in that society

00:53:25.770 --> 00:53:30.310
in order to understand the
role of that individual

00:53:30.310 --> 00:53:32.040
in the society.

00:53:32.040 --> 00:53:33.380
So that was very interesting.

00:53:33.380 --> 00:53:36.800
So the idea, again, I
remove structurally parts

00:53:36.800 --> 00:53:39.660
of the system and then see what happens

00:53:39.660 --> 00:53:41.970
in order to understand the function.

00:53:41.970 --> 00:53:44.280
And that's exactly like the idea

00:53:46.550 --> 00:53:49.010
that underlies gene knockouts.

00:53:49.010 --> 00:53:51.310
But I was puzzled by this

00:53:51.310 --> 00:53:55.110
whether we really have a
philosophical or conceptual basis

00:53:55.110 --> 00:53:56.510
for this approach.

00:53:56.510 --> 00:53:59.650
Of course, the idea is
if I remove something

00:53:59.650 --> 00:54:02.910
and the behavior changes
or the phenotype changes

00:54:02.910 --> 00:54:07.420
then I understand the function.

00:54:07.420 --> 00:54:09.940
That's basically the
idea but I was not sure

00:54:09.940 --> 00:54:12.120
whether there's some
conceptual foundation of it.

00:54:12.120 --> 00:54:14.100
And then I try to understand it

00:54:14.100 --> 00:54:17.460
in terms of Judea
Pearl's causality theory.

00:54:17.460 --> 00:54:21.740
And it seems to me that we
need more than intervention.

00:54:21.740 --> 00:54:26.740
We really structural
perturbation of the system,

00:54:26.810 --> 00:54:31.020
removal of parts, for
example, neurons in the brain

00:54:31.020 --> 00:54:36.020
really revealed some additional
information about the system

00:54:36.250 --> 00:54:39.820
that we cannot get simply by intervention.

00:54:39.820 --> 00:54:41.157
And this is why--

00:54:42.250 --> 00:54:43.630
- Yeah, and the interesting thing

00:54:43.630 --> 00:54:48.040
is that if I'm understanding it correctly

00:54:48.040 --> 00:54:52.890
that this knockout is not
actually loss of information.

00:54:52.890 --> 00:54:55.470
It is loss of a particular
kind of information

00:54:55.470 --> 00:54:56.940
that is going to be richer

00:54:56.940 --> 00:54:58.700
in terms of what it gives you for data.

00:54:58.700 --> 00:55:01.593
- Exactly, exactly, exactly, yeah.

00:55:04.230 --> 00:55:06.890
- Yeah, and I think this
last part maybe is that...

00:55:06.890 --> 00:55:10.320
So I agree about this kind of
complimentary set of diagrams

00:55:10.320 --> 00:55:13.340
with the different types of
experiments that are being done.

00:55:13.340 --> 00:55:16.350
And I think if you can go
back to the scientific method

00:55:16.350 --> 00:55:17.680
part of it, what's interesting

00:55:17.680 --> 00:55:22.680
is that when people try to
decide what experiment to do,

00:55:22.960 --> 00:55:25.400
you know, part of it is
about trying to come up

00:55:25.400 --> 00:55:28.860
with like the one that has
the most statistical power

00:55:28.860 --> 00:55:32.470
within some kind of just
at the association level

00:55:32.470 --> 00:55:35.710
but there's also a lot of
times a good experiments

00:55:35.710 --> 00:55:38.840
are kind of what reveal, would
be able to break a degeneracy

00:55:38.840 --> 00:55:41.600
between say two competing
mechanisms, right?

00:55:41.600 --> 00:55:43.170
So you have like two hypotheses

00:55:43.170 --> 00:55:45.140
for what the graphical
model might look like.

00:55:45.140 --> 00:55:46.090
They're both consistent

00:55:46.090 --> 00:55:47.910
with the data that you have thus far.

00:55:47.910 --> 00:55:49.130
And you're trying to build one

00:55:49.130 --> 00:55:51.550
that's going to try to like
make a sharp distinction

00:55:51.550 --> 00:55:52.920
between those two.

00:55:52.920 --> 00:55:56.910
And figuring out how to
design them, you know,

00:55:56.910 --> 00:55:59.280
a lot of times you can
just inspect the graph

00:55:59.280 --> 00:56:01.240
and kind of if you know a little
bit about what's going on,

00:56:01.240 --> 00:56:03.270
you have a pretty good idea
of what you should go do.

00:56:03.270 --> 00:56:06.470
And that's largely how
experimentalists have done things

00:56:06.470 --> 00:56:07.810
or just through intuition, you know,

00:56:07.810 --> 00:56:10.855
like, oh, well if I do this,
that's gonna happen, you know?

00:56:10.855 --> 00:56:12.220
And so that's pretty miraculous.

00:56:12.220 --> 00:56:13.490
And if you contrast that against something

00:56:13.490 --> 00:56:14.550
like reinforcement learning

00:56:14.550 --> 00:56:16.140
where you just like drive
the car off the cliff

00:56:16.140 --> 00:56:19.270
a million times until it's a
really good race car driver

00:56:19.270 --> 00:56:20.210
or something.

00:56:20.210 --> 00:56:21.860
They're very differential, right?

00:56:25.042 --> 00:56:26.457
But then the other part of that,

00:56:26.457 --> 00:56:29.210
the other side of that coin
is if you do an intervention

00:56:29.210 --> 00:56:32.180
and you get some data
and it doesn't conform

00:56:32.180 --> 00:56:35.490
to what you expect in your causal model,

00:56:35.490 --> 00:56:38.420
maybe any of them then you're
like what's going wrong.

00:56:38.420 --> 00:56:39.830
And like I can exclude that theory

00:56:39.830 --> 00:56:41.350
but that's not the end
of the story, right?

00:56:41.350 --> 00:56:44.400
You then start generating new hypotheses

00:56:44.400 --> 00:56:45.530
and you start trying to figure out

00:56:45.530 --> 00:56:47.910
like what is the new mechanism

00:56:47.910 --> 00:56:51.523
that could potentially
respond in the way that I saw.

00:56:52.864 --> 00:56:57.680
And again that imagination
and the hypothesis building

00:56:57.680 --> 00:56:58.520
is not at the level

00:56:58.520 --> 00:57:00.740
of like running a lot
more low-level simulations

00:57:00.740 --> 00:57:03.800
it's at this very like
high-level, you know,

00:57:03.800 --> 00:57:05.820
big building block course grand picture

00:57:05.820 --> 00:57:06.983
of what's going on.

00:57:08.299 --> 00:57:10.790
And yeah, so again I think it's a place

00:57:10.790 --> 00:57:13.780
where you see like that's super
core to how we think about,

00:57:13.780 --> 00:57:15.880
like children playing.

00:57:15.880 --> 00:57:17.130
It's all about that.

00:57:17.130 --> 00:57:22.130
I pushed the thing off the
table a thousand times and yeah.

00:57:23.240 --> 00:57:26.406
So that interplay is
really interesting to me.

00:57:26.406 --> 00:57:27.239
- Right.

00:57:27.239 --> 00:57:30.400
And then I think also
in the cases of building

00:57:30.400 --> 00:57:33.633
these kinds of physical
models driven by physics,

00:57:34.670 --> 00:57:36.490
I mean, those are the
places Kyle as you mentioned

00:57:36.490 --> 00:57:38.010
where it could be revealed

00:57:38.010 --> 00:57:40.980
that you actually have
picked the wrong variables.

00:57:40.980 --> 00:57:43.970
You know, these are not the
characteristic variables

00:57:43.970 --> 00:57:48.620
that actually are salient to
the dynamics of the system

00:57:48.620 --> 00:57:51.050
or to predict behavior, right?

00:57:51.050 --> 00:57:54.280
So I think sometimes you get stuck in that

00:57:54.280 --> 00:57:56.260
where you suddenly do realize

00:57:56.260 --> 00:58:01.260
that when none of the causal
networks actually works

00:58:02.250 --> 00:58:04.010
to explain the data

00:58:04.010 --> 00:58:07.080
that perhaps what you have actually chosen

00:58:07.080 --> 00:58:10.337
to generate the data are not
the right variables, right?

00:58:10.337 --> 00:58:14.130
And they don't actually
capture all the features

00:58:14.130 --> 00:58:15.377
of the model.

00:58:16.350 --> 00:58:17.220
- Yeah, yeah.

00:58:17.220 --> 00:58:18.840
I mean, the other thing
I like about that picture

00:58:18.840 --> 00:58:21.480
is when you think about
reductionism and emergence

00:58:21.480 --> 00:58:23.745
and things like this, you
can kind of think of them

00:58:23.745 --> 00:58:28.620
as different causal models
at different scales of that.

00:58:28.620 --> 00:58:30.040
You're thinking about a system

00:58:30.040 --> 00:58:31.410
and maybe they're all connected

00:58:31.410 --> 00:58:34.190
but the way the phenomenon
manifest themselves

00:58:34.190 --> 00:58:35.690
look drastically different, right?

00:58:35.690 --> 00:58:37.180
At the different scales.

00:58:37.180 --> 00:58:41.560
And ultimately if you could
somehow understand the mapping

00:58:41.560 --> 00:58:45.090
between like the more the
reductionist causal model

00:58:45.090 --> 00:58:46.610
and the emergent one,

00:58:46.610 --> 00:58:48.580
like that's a fantastic
if you can do that.

00:58:48.580 --> 00:58:51.790
And very occasionally we
can do things like that

00:58:51.790 --> 00:58:54.520
with like quarks and gluons

00:58:54.520 --> 00:58:58.610
to protons to atoms and molecules.

00:58:58.610 --> 00:59:02.250
And like occasionally
we can make that bridge

00:59:02.250 --> 00:59:06.560
but if we could get a lot better at that

00:59:08.120 --> 00:59:09.510
and I think that's what
the kind of significance

00:59:09.510 --> 00:59:12.680
of a lot of this machinery is,

00:59:12.680 --> 00:59:15.713
it's like kind of the necessary
step towards that path.

